===Alternative forms===
* [[canuck]]

===Etymology===
1835 ''Kanuk'' (US), 1849 ''Canuck'' (Canadian), origin unclear. Several dictionaries simply state that it is an alteration of {{term|Canada}} or {{term|Canadian}}.

Several theories hold that the name began as an informal self-appellation by an early Canadian minority, and later acquired a national identity. A few sources explain the ending as coming from {{etyl|iu}} {{term|inuk||man, person|lang=iu}}, from {{term|Chinook||Aboriginal people of the U.S. Pacific Northwest}}, or another Canadian Aboriginal ending like {{term||-uc}}, {{term||-uq}}, or {{term||-oc}}.

A number of linguists are certain that the name comes from {{etyl|haw}} {{term|kanaka||man|lang=haw}}, a self-appellation of indentured colonial [[canoeman|canoemen]] and Hawaiian sailors working off the Pacific Northwest, the Arctic, and New England coasts.  The term may have come to English through {{etyl|fr}} {{term|canaque|lang=fr}}, or more likely, via American [[whalers]].  Compare English {{term|Kanak}} and French {{term|Kanak|lang=fr}} or {{term|canaque|lang=fr}}, Austrian German {{term|Kanake|lang=de}}.

Another theory is that the name is from the surname {{term||Connaught}}, used as a French-Canadian nickname for the Irish.  Yet another speculates that the origin is {{etyl|lre}} {{term|kanata||village|lang=lre}}, which is also the origin of {{term|Canada}}.

Compare obsolete spellings {{term|Canack}}, {{term|Cannack}}, {{term|Canuk}}, {{term|Conuck}}, {{term|Cunnuck}}, {{term|Kanuck}}, {{term|Kanuk}}, {{term|K'nuck}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/kəˈnʌk/}}
* {{hyphenation|Can|uck}}
* {{audio|En-ca-canuck.ogg|Audio (CA)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{Canada|informal}} A [[Canadian]].
#: ''I am a sixth generation '''Canuck''', and proud of it.''
# {{US|slang|often|derogatory}} A Canadian, especially a [[French Canadian]].
# {{sports}} A member of the [[Vancouver]] Canucks professional [[NHL]] [[ice hockey]] team.
# The name of the [[w:Avro Canada CF-100|Avro Canada CF-100]] fighter-interceptor.
# {{US|obsolete}} A Canadian [[pony]] or [[horse]].

====Usage notes====
In Canada, the term is not derogatory, and considered to apply to all Canadians.  In the United States the term is often considered derogatory, and especially offensive to French Canadians in New England.

